<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n12127" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἄπιος</span></b>  (B), <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">η</span>,
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>far away, far off, distant,</i>
			
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τηλόθεν ἐξ ἀπίης γαίης</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Il.
					1.270
				</span></i>
			, <i><span class="en">
				3.49
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Od.
				16.18
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>OC</u>
				1685
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἄπιος, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">α</span>,
					<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>,</span>
			<i>Apian,</i> i.e. <i>Peloponnesian,</i> said (in this sense) to
			be derived from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἆπις,</span> Apis, a
			mythical king of Argos, <i><span class="en">
				A.
				<u>Supp.</u>
				260
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Paus.
				2.5.7
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἀπία γῆ, Ἀπία χθών,</span>
			or <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἀπία</span> alone, <i>the
				Peloponnese,</i> esp. <i>Argolis,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				A.
				<u>Ag.</u>
				256
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>OC</u>
				1303
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ath.
				14.650b
			</span></i>, etc.; cf.<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἀπίς</span> prob. in
				<i><span class="en">
				A.R.
				4.1564
			</span></i>. [The former word has <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ᾰ</span>, the
			latter <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ᾱ;</span> yet <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>OC</u>
				1685
			</span></i> uses signf. <i><span class="en">
				1
			</span></i> with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ᾱ</span>, and later 
				Ep.
			 Poets have signf. <i><span class="en">
				11
			</span></i> with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ᾰ</span>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Rhian.
				13
			</span></i>.] (Commonly derived from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀπό,</span> as <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀντίος</span> from
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀντί;</span> and <i><span class="en">
				Hsch.
			</span></i> expl. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐξ ἀπίης γαίης</span> by
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀλλοτρίας ἢ ξένης ἢ μακρὰν
				οὔσης,</span> cf. <i><span class="en">
				Str.
				8.6.9
			</span></i>.)</div>
